This is the classic recurrence f(n) = max(f(n//2) + f(n//3) + f(n//4) + f(n//5), n), using integer division.

Check with n=60: f(30)+f(20)+f(15)+f(12).
- f(12) = max(f(6)+f(4)+f(3)+f(2), 12). Here f(6) = max(f(3)+f(2)+f(1)+f(1), 6), and f(3) = max(1+1+0+0, 3) = 3, f(2) = 2, f(1) = 1. So f(6) = max(3+2+1+1, 6) = 7. f(4) = max(f(2)+f(1)+f(1)+f(0), 4) = max(2+1+1+0, 4) = 4. So f(12) = max(7+4+3+2, 12) = 16.
- f(15) = f(7)+f(5)+f(3)+f(3). f(7) = f(3)+f(2)+f(1)+f(1) = 7, so it is max(7, 7) = 7. f(5) = f(2)+f(1)+f(1)+f(1) = 5, so it is 5. f(15) = max(7+5+3+3, 15) = 18.
- f(20) = f(10)+f(6)+f(5)+f(4). f(10) = f(5)+f(3)+f(2)+f(2) = 5+3+2+2 = 12, so it is 12. f(20) = max(12+7+5+4, 20) = 28.
- f(30) = f(15)+f(10)+f(7)+f(6) = 18+12+7+7 = 44, so f(30) = 44.

Total: 44+28+18+16 = 106, which matches the assert.

Base case: f(0) = 0, which follows from max(0, 0). The code returns n for n < 1 to avoid infinite recursion, and memoizes results.

